  <div id="sutta">
  
            <h4 id="StNp4.8" class="h1">4:8  To Pasūra</h4>
            <div class="verse">
              <p>“‘Only here is there purity’</p>
              <p class="v1">—that’s what they say—</p>
              <p>‘No other doctrines are pure’</p>
              <p class="v1">—so they say.</p>
              <p>Insisting that what they depend on is good,</p>
              <p>they are deeply entrenched</p>
              <p>in idiosyncratic truths.<span class="fn"><a href="#stnp_note4.8.01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Seeking controversy, they plunge into an assembly,</p>
                <p>regarding one another as fools.</p>
                <p>Relying on others’ authority,</p>
                <p>they speak in debate.</p>
                <p>Desiring praise, they claim to be skilled.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Engaged in disputes in the midst of the assembly,</p>
                <p class="v1">—anxious, desiring praise—</p>
                <p>the one defeated is</p>
                <p class="v1">chagrined.</p>
                <p>Shaken with criticism, he seeks for an opening.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>He whose doctrine is [judged as] demolished,</p>
                <p class="v1">defeated, by those judging the issue:</p>
                <p>He laments, he grieves—the inferior exponent.</p>
                <p class="v1">‘He beat me,’ he mourns.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>These disputes have arisen among contemplatives.</p>
                <p>In them are</p>
                <p class="v5">elation,</p>
                <p class="v5">dejection.</p>
                <p>Seeing this, one should abstain from disputes,</p>
                <p class="v1">for they have no other goal</p>
                <p class="v1">than the gaining of praise.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>While he who is praised there</p>
                <p class="v1">for expounding his doctrine</p>
                <p class="v1">in the midst of the assembly,</p>
                <p>laughs on that account &amp; grows haughty,</p>
                <p class="v1">attaining his heart’s desire.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>That haughtiness will be grounds for his damage,</p>
                <p class="v1">for he’ll speak in pride &amp; conceit.</p>
                <p>Seeing this, one should abstain from debates.</p>
                <p>No purity is attained by them, say the skilled.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Like a strong man nourished on royal food,</p>
                <p>you prowl about, roaring, searching out an opponent.</p>
                <p>Wherever the battle is,</p>
                <p class="v1">go there, strong man.</p>
                <p>As before, there’s none here.</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Those who dispute, taking hold of a view,</p>
                <p>saying, “This, and this only, is true,”</p>
                <p class="v1">those you can talk to.</p>
                <p>Here there is nothing—</p>
                <p class="v1">no confrontation</p>
                <p class="v1">at the birth of disputes.<span class="fn"><a href="#stnp_note4.8.02">2</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>Whom would you gain as opponent, Pasūra,</p>
                <p>among those who live above confrontation—</p>
                <p class="v1">not pitting view against view—</p>
                <p class="v1">who have nothing here grasped as supreme?</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
              <div class="verse-add">
                <p>So here you come,</p>
                <p class="v1">conjecturing,</p>
                <p>your mind thinking up</p>
                <p class="v1">viewpoints.</p>
                <p>You’re paired off with a pure one</p>
                <p class="v1">and so cannot proceed.”</p>
              </div><!--end verse-add-->
            </div><!--end verse-->
            <p class="suttaCite">vv. 824–834</p><!--end suttaCite-->
            <div class="note">
              <p class="notetitle">Notes</p><!--end notetitle-->
              <p id="stnp_note4.8.01">1. <em>Pacceka-sacca.</em> The word <em>pacceka</em> can also mean singular, personal, or individual. <a href="../../AN/AN10_20.html">AN 10:20</a> lists the following views as idiosyncratic truths. “The cosmos is eternal,” “The cosmos is not eternal,” “The cosmos is finite,” “The cosmos is infinite,” “The soul &amp; the body are the same,” “The soul is one thing &amp; the body another,” “After death a Tathāgata exists,” “After death a Tathāgata does not exist,” “After death a Tathāgata both does &amp; does not exist,” “After death a Tathāgata neither does nor does not exist.” These truths are distinct from noble truths, in that the word for “noble”—<em>ariya</em>—can also mean “universal.”</p>
              <p id="stnp_note4.8.02">2. See <a href="../../AN/AN10_93.html">AN 10:93</a>.</p>
              <p>On the Buddha as a debater, see <em>Skill in Questions,</em> chapter 5.</p>
            </div><!--end note-->
            <p class="seealso">See also: <a href="../../MN/MN18.html">MN 18</a>; <a href="../../MN/MN36.html">MN 36</a>; <a href="../../MN/MN58.html">MN 58</a>; <a href="../../MN/MN101.html">MN 101</a>; <a href="../../SN/SN42_8.html">SN 42:8</a>; <a href="../../AN/AN2_36.html">AN 2:36</a>; <a href="../../AN/AN3_62.html">AN 3:62</a>; <a href="../../AN/AN3_68.html">AN 3:68</a>; <a href="../../AN/AN3_73.html">AN 3:73</a></p>
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
